  "result": "**Cause:** default args evaluated once, at `def` time. `items=[]` makes one list object, stored on function (`add.__defaults__`). Every call without `items` reuses same list. First call appends 1 \u2192 `[1]`. Second call appends 2 to same list \u2192 `[1, 2]`.\n\n**Fix:** use `None` sentinel, make new list inside:\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` \u2192 `[1]`, `add(2)` \u2192 `[2]`.\n\nSame trap for any mutable default: `{}`, `set()`, custom objects. Linters flag it (pylint `W0102`, ruff `B006`).",
